1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,199
Welcome to Cricket X-Ray:

2
00:00:02,199 --> 00:00:07,758
discovering cricket player performance
profiles with unsupervised machine learning.

3
00:00:07,808 --> 00:00:13,474
A Milestone 3 project by Arjav Jain,
Nishkarsh Sisodia and Drishti Chauhan.

4
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:18,316
Cricket players are usually
described by informal roles:

5
00:00:18,316 --> 00:00:21,674
batter, bowler, all-rounder and wicket-keeper.

6
00:00:21,724 --> 00:00:28,065
But roles overlap: many batters also bowl,
and many keepers are leading batters.

7
00:00:28,115 --> 00:00:34,013
There is no reliable role label to learn
from, so this is an unsupervised problem.

8
00:00:34,063 --> 00:00:37,685
Our goal: group T20 players
by how they contribute,

9
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:42,977
using career statistics alone, and
explain each group in cricket terms.

10
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:49,698
We use three T20 international career
tables: batting, bowling and fielding.

11
00:00:49,748 --> 00:00:55,733
Each holds the same 2,006 players, merged
on the full name with its country tag.

12
00:00:55,783 --> 00:01:01,842
A dash means missing, not zero: a player
who never bowled has no economy rate.

13
00:01:01,892 --> 00:01:09,991
And overs are not decimals: 10.5 overs
is 10 overs and 5 balls, or 65 balls.

14
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:12,782
Here is the pipeline.

15
00:01:12,832 --> 00:01:19,715
We load and validate the files into one
table of 2,006 players and 44 columns.

16
00:01:19,765 --> 00:01:24,057
We keep the 1,105 players
with at least five matches,

17
00:01:24,057 --> 00:01:28,517
and engineer twelve per-innings
and per-match rates.

18
00:01:28,567 --> 00:01:33,235
For the model, we clip each feature
to its 1st to 99th percentile,

19
00:01:33,235 --> 00:01:38,930
fill undefined rates with the median,
add a has-bowled flag, and standardize.

20
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:41,021
K-Means groups the players.

21
00:01:41,071 --> 00:01:43,851
PCA maps them in two dimensions.

22
00:01:43,901 --> 00:01:47,539
And simple rules give each
cluster a readable label.

23
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,382
Now, the live Streamlit dashboard.

24
00:01:51,434 --> 00:01:57,112
The sidebar sets the minimum matches,
5, and the number of clusters K, 4.

25
00:01:57,164 --> 00:02:03,566
The Overview shows 2,006 source
players, 1,105 modelled players,

26
00:02:03,566 --> 00:02:08,818
9 model features, and a
silhouette score of 0.269.

27
00:02:08,870 --> 00:02:13,285
Below are a PCA preview and
the size of each cluster.

28
00:02:13,938 --> 00:02:16,312
The Data tab checks the inputs.

29
00:02:16,363 --> 00:02:20,764
Three source files, 2,006
rows each, no duplicates,

30
00:02:20,764 --> 00:02:24,293
and every player appears in all three.

31
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:29,192
Here are the missing values per
column; a dash always stays missing.

32
00:02:29,845 --> 00:02:37,135
In Features, the filter removes 901
players with fewer than five matches,

33
00:02:37,135 --> 00:02:38,783
leaving 1,105.

34
00:02:38,835 --> 00:02:44,031
We engineer twelve rates in three
groups: batting, bowling and fielding.

35
00:02:44,082 --> 00:02:47,029
The definitions table gives each formula.

36
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:52,396
Eight of these rates, plus the has-bowled
flag, form the nine model inputs.

37
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:57,636
Model evaluation fits K-Means
for every K from 2 to 8.

38
00:02:57,687 --> 00:03:06,824
The elbow bends most sharply at K = 3, while
the silhouette peaks at K = 4, at 0.269.

39
00:03:06,875 --> 00:03:13,547
We chose K = 4: the best silhouette, and
four clusters that are easy to interpret.

40
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:19,202
A score this modest means weak structure,
as expected, because roles overlap.

41
00:03:19,857 --> 00:03:23,555
The Clusters tab shows the result for K = 4.

42
00:03:23,606 --> 00:03:31,440
The PCA map keeps 51.6% of the variance;
each cluster has its own colour and marker.

43
00:03:31,491 --> 00:03:39,338
The four profiles: Bowling-Oriented, 463
players; Batting-Oriented Also Bowls,

44
00:03:39,338 --> 00:03:46,127
320; Batting-Oriented Rarely Bowls,
216; and Wicket-Keeping, 106.

45
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:49,554
The profile table gives
the real median statistics.

46
00:03:49,606 --> 00:03:52,471
The heatmap shows relative strengths,

47
00:03:52,471 --> 00:03:57,059
where the keepers stand out
at +3.0 standard deviations.

48
00:03:57,110 --> 00:04:00,465
And the results can be downloaded as CSV.

49
00:04:01,118 --> 00:04:04,863
The Player explorer looks
at one player at a time.

50
00:04:04,914 --> 00:04:07,666
Let's search for V Kohli of India.

51
00:04:07,717 --> 00:04:11,900
He falls in Cluster 2,
Batting-Oriented, Also Bowls.

52
00:04:11,951 --> 00:04:15,700
This panel shows that Cluster
2 is his nearest centre,

53
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,074
and compares his standardized values with it.

54
00:04:19,125 --> 00:04:24,351
Below are his source statistics,
exactly as in the CSV files.

55
00:04:24,402 --> 00:04:27,716
And here are his five most similar players.

56
00:04:28,368 --> 00:04:30,714
Every tab follows the sidebar.

57
00:04:30,765 --> 00:04:33,381
Let's change K from 4 to 3.

58
00:04:33,432 --> 00:04:36,731
The model retrains at once: the metrics,

59
00:04:36,731 --> 00:04:43,414
the PCA map and the cluster sizes all
update, and the silhouette is now 0.265.

60
00:04:43,465 --> 00:04:46,280
Clusters now shows three profiles.

61
00:04:47,664 --> 00:04:51,701
Back to K = 4, and the four profiles return.

62
00:04:52,354 --> 00:04:56,621
Finally, the Methodology tab
explains every step in plain words,

63
00:04:56,621 --> 00:04:59,471
from the problem to the cluster labels.

64
00:04:59,522 --> 00:05:04,144
It ends with the model inputs,
the limitations and future work.

65
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:11,734
The project has 80 automated tests, written
with unittest and Streamlit's AppTest.

66
00:05:11,784 --> 00:05:16,518
They cover data loading, preprocessing,
modeling and the dashboard.

67
00:05:16,568 --> 00:05:19,790
With the real data present, all 80 pass.

68
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:26,344
Without the data, as on GitHub
Actions, 52 pass and 28 skip by design.

69
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:29,842
Real players have no true labels,

70
00:05:29,842 --> 00:05:35,049
so we also built a synthetic league
of 60 players, 15 of each type.

71
00:05:35,099 --> 00:05:40,640
With K = 4, K-Means recovered
all four types with 100% purity,

72
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:43,392
and every label was correct.

73
00:05:43,442 --> 00:05:50,214
Its silhouette of 0.583 shows that the modest
real score comes from overlapping roles,

74
00:05:50,214 --> 00:05:52,197
not from the pipeline.

75
00:05:53,233 --> 00:05:55,681
To conclude: with no labels,

76
00:05:55,681 --> 00:06:02,224
Cricket X-Ray found four interpretable
profiles among 1,105 T20 players.

77
00:06:02,274 --> 00:06:06,403
The 0.269 silhouette shows
that roles form a continuum,

78
00:06:06,403 --> 00:06:10,632
and the synthetic test shows
that the pipeline works.

79
00:06:10,682 --> 00:06:14,151
Limits: career totals ignore batting position,

80
00:06:14,151 --> 00:06:18,247
era and form, and K-Means
assumes compact clusters.

81
00:06:18,297 --> 00:06:21,229
Next, we would add ODI and Test data,

82
00:06:21,229 --> 00:06:25,363
try Gaussian mixtures,
and use ball-by-ball data.

83
00:06:25,413 --> 00:06:28,701
The code is on GitHub. Thank you!
